I put some thought into a system like that some time ago, but
eventually abandoned it.

First, let me say I'm confident such a system could work.  What you'd
have to do is find a way to generalize magic effects in order to apply
to a wide variety of situations, and then make up rules detailing how
they would be implemented.  We already have something like this in the
special effects for blessed and cursed items, and
reading-while-confused.  If you did it right you wouldn't necessarily
have to special-code each case.

For example, let's say you knew the "syllables" for effect:fire,
range:small area, and power:double-strength.  Each "range" would be a
different loop that iterated over each spot on the map that the spell
affected (though it need not be implemented exactly like that), what's
done during that iteration would be the routine for that effect, and
the power could possibly be a changable variable in the calculations
for each possible effect.

Of course, not all spells are elemental effect, and the challenge in
making this work would be in generalizing the concept enough so that
you could also have a wide variety of effects, like teleport, detect
monster, polymorph, genocide, etc., that could also be slotted in, and
that would be harder.

I still think it's possible to put this into a game and have it work,
but I'm not sure that game should be Nethack.  Because this results
in, basically, a perfect mirroring of effects, which diminishes the
uniqueness of each spell.  If fire is a drop-in replacement for ice,
then what's the reason for using one over another?  Some monsters are
fire-resistant and/or cold resistant I suppose, but I don't think
that's enough.  Nethack now has special fireball and cold effects
for skilled spellcasters that differentiate between the two powers,
and I like that idea.

Of course it's always possible to put differences in as special cases
(fire gets explosions sprinkled around the range instead of a fire
effect for each space, cold maybe turns the ground within range slick
temporarily) and of course there's always the special item damages
(boiling/freezing potions, burning scrolls, exploding rings), but that
decreases the elegance of the system and multiplies the amount of
coding required to make it work.

